The question lists the following criteria for a set to be compact:
(a) the image of a compact set under a continuous map is compact;
(b) any finite union of compact sets is compact;
(c) any closed subset of a compact set is compact;
(d) any bounded closed set in Euclidean space is compact;
(e) any product of compact spaces is compact.

From the Brouwer invariance of domain proof in the exam context, let $f: \overline{B^n} \to \mathbb{R}^n$ be an injective continuous map, $c \in \mathbb{R}^n \setminus f(\overline{B^n})$ with $|c - f(0)| < \varepsilon$, and define
$$\Sigma_1 = \{y \in f(\overline{B^n}) : |y - c| \geqslant \varepsilon\}, \qquad \Sigma_2 = \{y \in \mathbb{R}^n : |y - c| = \varepsilon\}, \qquad \Sigma = \Sigma_1 \bigcup \Sigma_2.$$
Explain why each of the four underlined sets $\overline{B^n}$, $f(\overline{B^n})$, $\Sigma_1$, $\Sigma$ is compact (state which criterion applies).
